eulers-equations-of-motion.symmetry_origin:
Euler's equations are derived from Newton's second law for rotation, \f(\frac{d\boldsymbol{L}}{dt} = \boldsymbol{N}\f), where \f(\boldsymbol{L}\f) is the angular momentum and \f(\boldsymbol{N}\f) is the net torque. The key step is expressing the time derivative \f(\frac{d\boldsymbol{L}}{dt}\f) in a rotating, body-fixed reference frame. If \f(\boldsymbol{L}_{body}\f) is the angular momentum vector expressed in the body frame and \f(\boldsymbol{\boldsymbol{
u}}}\f) is the angular velocity of the body frame relative to an inertial frame, then \f(\frac{d\boldsymbol{L}}{dt}\f)_{inertial} = (\frac{d\boldsymbol{L}_{body}}{dt})_{body} + \boldsymbol{\boldsymbol{
u}}} 	imes \boldsymbol{L}_{body}\f). Substituting \f(\boldsymbol{L}_{body} = \boldsymbol{I} \boldsymbol{\boldsymbol{
u}}}\f) and setting \f(\frac{d\boldsymbol{L}}{dt}\f)_{inertial} = \boldsymbol{N}\f) yields Euler's equations. The formulation intrinsically handles the coordinate transformation between inertial and body-fixed frames, reflecting the rotational symmetry of physical laws.

